===== configuring-the-jail/SKILL.md =====
---
name: configuring-the-jail
description: "Change applied host environment settings."
---

# Host environment

## Apply a host configuration change

1. Change the user TOML at `/home/test/.config/yolo-jail/config.jsonc`, or, when that file is generated from a source of the user's config (a dotfiles repository, say), edit that source, not the installed host config; if the source is absent, ask the human. Use `yolo config-ref` for accepted keys and their scope.
2. Run `yolo check` from `/home/test/project` and resolve every reported failure.
3. Run `yolo host apply` from `/home/test/project`. Its output is the record of what changed.

## Select skills in user TOML

Direct scoped pack selection is user-only. Use `yolo pack add local/corpus --skills-dir skills/core-tools --only skill-a --dry-run` to inspect a raw selection, then omit `--dry-run` to write the entry `{ pack = "local/corpus", skills = { dir = "skills/core-tools", only = ["skill-a"] } }`. Omit `--skills-dir` for typed candidate names. `--subdir` alone imports a whole pack. `yolo pack explain local/corpus` reports every tuple without fetching or writing; unavailable data is unverified. Scoped entries activate no non-skill capabilities. Do not convert legacy file filters to name selectors by guesswork. Indexed config edits or exact roots disambiguate removal; these are user choices across workspaces, not this project's config or workspace pack authority.

===== diagnosing-the-jail/SKILL.md =====
---
name: diagnosing-the-jail
description: "Diagnose host configuration and apply failures."
---

# Diagnose host configuration

## 1. Validate before applying

Run `yolo check` from `/home/test/project`. Resolve each `[FAIL]` before applying; the check output names the config key and remedy.

## 2. Inspect the host apply

Run `yolo host apply` from `/home/test/project` and read its report for the exact files and skills applied. If it reports a failure, inspect the host log at `/home/test/.local/share/yolo-jail/host-apply.log` and address the named source or permission problem before retrying.

